GIT FUNCTIONS - COMPLETE GUIDE
================================

1. INITIALIZE A REPOSITORY
--------------------------
Command: git init
Explanation: Creates a new Git repository in your current directory. This creates 
a .git folder that tracks all changes to your project. Use this when starting a 
new project locally.
Usage: git init


2. CLONE A REPOSITORY
---------------------
Command: git clone <repository-url>
Explanation: Downloads an existing repository from GitHub to your local machine. 
This copies the entire project history and all branches. Use this to get a project 
that already exists on GitHub.
Usage: git clone https://github.com/yourusername/repo-name.git


3. STAGE CHANGES
----------------
Command: git add <filename> or git add .
Explanation: Prepares files for commit by moving them to the "staging area". 
This tells Git which changes you want to include in your next commit. You can 
stage specific files or all changes at once.
Usage: 
  git add filename.txt     (stage a specific file)
  git add .                (stage all changes)


4. COMMIT CHANGES
-----------------
Command: git commit -m "Your message"
Explanation: Saves staged changes with a description. A commit is a snapshot of 
your project at a specific point in time. Write clear, descriptive messages that 
explain what you changed (e.g., "Add login feature" or "Fix bug in parser").
Usage: git commit -m "Add user authentication"


5. PUSH TO GITHUB
-----------------
Command: git push origin main
Explanation: Uploads your local commits to GitHub. "origin" refers to your remote 
repository on GitHub, and "main" is the branch you're pushing to. This makes your 
changes available to others and backs up your work.
Usage: git push origin main


6. PULL FROM GITHUB
-------------------
Command: git pull origin main
Explanation: Downloads and merges changes from GitHub into your local branch. Use 
this to sync with team members' work or your own updates made on another machine. 
It combines "git fetch" and "git merge" into one command.
Usage: git pull origin main


7. CHECK STATUS
---------------
Command: git status
Explanation: Shows which files are modified, staged, or untracked. This is one of 
the most useful commands to understand what's changed in your project and what Git 
is tracking. Use this frequently before committing.
Usage: git status


ADDITIONAL USEFUL COMMANDS
===========================

8. VIEW COMMIT HISTORY
----------------------
Command: git log
Explanation: Displays all commits in your repository with timestamps and messages. 
Helps you understand the project's history and who made what changes.
Usage: git log


9. CREATE/LIST BRANCHES
-----------------------
Command: git branch or git branch <branch-name>
Explanation: Lists all branches (when used alone) or creates a new branch (with name). 
Branches allow you to work on different features without affecting the main code.
Usage: 
  git branch              (list all branches)
  git branch new-feature  (create a new branch)


10. SWITCH TO A BRANCH
----------------------
Command: git checkout -b <branch-name>
Explanation: Creates a new branch AND switches to it in one command. The -b flag 
means "create and checkout". Use this when starting work on a new feature.
Usage: git checkout -b feature/login-page


11. MERGE BRANCHES
------------------
Command: git merge <branch-name>
Explanation: Combines changes from one branch into your current branch. Use this 
when you've finished working on a feature and want to merge it back to main.
Usage: git merge feature/login-page


12. VIEW ALL BRANCHES (LOCAL AND REMOTE)
----------------------------------------
Command: git branch -a
Explanation: Shows all branches - both local branches (on your machine) and remote 
branches (on GitHub). This helps you see the complete branch structure and identify 
which branches exist. Remote branches are prefixed with "origin/".
Usage: git branch -a


13. SWITCH TO EXISTING BRANCH
------------------------------
Command: git checkout <branch-name>
Explanation: Switches from your current branch to another branch. This updates your 
working directory to match the selected branch. Use this when you want to work on 
a different branch or switch back to main.
Usage: git checkout main


14. DELETE A BRANCH LOCALLY
---------------------------
Command: git branch -d <branch-name>
Explanation: Deletes a branch from your local machine. Use -d to safely delete 
(only works if merged), or -D to force delete. This is useful when you've finished 
with a feature branch and no longer need it.
Usage: 
  git branch -d feature/login-page     (safe delete)
  git branch -D feature/login-page     (force delete)


15. DELETE A BRANCH ON GITHUB
-----------------------------
Command: git push origin --delete <branch-name>
Explanation: Removes a branch from your remote GitHub repository. Use this after 
deleting the branch locally to keep GitHub in sync. This prevents clutter in your 
repository.
Usage: git push origin --delete master


16. VIEW ALL COMMITS ON ALL BRANCHES
------------------------------------
Command: git log --all --oneline
Explanation: Displays all commits from all branches in a compact one-line format. 
This is useful for troubleshooting when commits seem to be missing - they might 
exist on a different branch. The --all flag shows everything.
Usage: git log --all --oneline


17. COPY COMMITS FROM ONE BRANCH TO ANOTHER
--------------------------------------------
Command: git cherry-pick <commit-hash>
Explanation: Takes a specific commit from one branch and applies it to your current 
branch. Useful when you need only certain commits from another branch without 
merging everything. Get the commit hash from git log.
Usage: git cherry-pick abc123def


TROUBLESHOOTING: TWO BRANCHES (MAIN AND MASTER)
================================================

Problem: You have both "main" and "master" branches, or can't see your first commit.

Solution: Keep only main and merge everything into it:

Step 1: View all commits and branches
  git log --all --oneline
  git branch -a

Step 2: Switch to main branch
  git checkout main

Step 3: Merge master into main (if commits are on master)
  git merge master

Step 4: Delete the master branch locally
  git branch -d master

Step 5: Delete master from GitHub
  git push origin --delete master

Step 6: Push main to GitHub
  git push origin main

Step 7: Verify everything is correct
  git log
  git branch -a


QUICK START WORKFLOW
====================

Step 1: Create or navigate to your project folder
  cd your-project

Step 2: Initialize Git
  git init

Step 3: Connect to GitHub (after creating a repo on GitHub)
  git remote add origin https://github.com/yourusername/repo-name.git

Step 4: Make changes to files
  (edit your files using your editor)

Step 5: Stage and commit
  git add .
  git commit -m "Initial commit"

Step 6: Push to GitHub
  git push -u origin main


IMPORTANT TIP
=============
Create a .gitignore file in your project to exclude files from being tracked:
- node_modules/  (for Node.js projects)
- .env           (for environment variables)
- __pycache__/   (for Python projects)
- .DS_Store      (for macOS)

This prevents unwanted files from being uploaded to GitHub.
